From 489891fae218263cf1551beab6f0dcd965300bb6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 22:23:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: Dolby Vision sent as HDR10+ on nvidia-drm,
 with metadata in the commit of the mode where the AVI InfoFrame would change

On GTX 16 to RTX 40 graphics and with driver 580, where NVIDIA's driver
names a 4K mode by an HDMI VIC, the AVI InfoFrame carries the CTA VIC of
the mode once HDR10+ goes out (nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9050,
nvidia-legacy-open-9062), and keeps it while the mode stays. A film's
own HDR10+ starts in the commit of the mode, so that change goes out
with the mode. Dolby Vision sent as HDR10+ has no metadata there yet:
the AVI InfoFrame changed a frame or more after the mode, at the first
HDR10+ frame, which a display may take as a new signal.

Where the connector says that its driver does that
(NV_HDR10PLUS_AVI_CTA_VIC, nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9052,
nvidia-legacy-open-9063: never on RTX 50, whose HDR10+ has a slot of its
own) and the mode and the display are those where it does
(KODI::UTILS::NvkmsAviCtaVicWithHdr10Plus(), utils/EdidHdmiVic.h: the
rule of NVKMS read from the raw EDID, which gives NVKMS's own answer for
every 4K timing of 593 real EDIDs), the first picture of the video
waits for its own measurement as on i915 (two periods of the mode, 100
ms at most) and goes in the commit of the mode, with provisional
metadata from its level 1 block where the measurement does not come in
time. The AVI InfoFrame then changes with the mode and never after it.
Every other mode, display and driver keeps the requests of 1.0 RC4.

The log of the wait names the reason for each driver.
